1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,406
What if one of Europe's most celebrated

2
00:00:03,406 --> 00:00:05,953
tech companies said it had billions in

3
00:00:05,953 --> 00:00:06,692
the bank,

4
00:00:06,692 --> 00:00:07,430
and then,

5
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:08,169
one day,

6
00:00:08,169 --> 00:00:11,479
admitted that the money had probably never

7
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:12,843
existed?

8
00:00:18,083 --> 00:00:21,013
That happened in June twenty twenty.

9
00:00:21,113 --> 00:00:23,953
The German payments company Wirecard said

10
00:00:23,953 --> 00:00:27,409
that cash worth about 2.2 billion dollars,

11
00:00:27,409 --> 00:00:30,013
converted at that year's average exchange

12
00:00:30,013 --> 00:00:30,463
rate,

13
00:00:30,463 --> 00:00:33,193
was most likely not there at all.

14
00:00:33,393 --> 00:00:34,282
Days later,

15
00:00:34,282 --> 00:00:35,743
it was bankrupt.

16
00:00:35,743 --> 00:00:38,490
Germany's own finance minister would later

17
00:00:38,490 --> 00:00:40,551
call it the country's greatest postwar

18
00:00:40,551 --> 00:00:42,066
accounting fraud.

19
00:00:47,717 --> 00:00:49,963
So how does a small payments firm

20
00:00:49,963 --> 00:00:52,489
near Munich become a star of the

21
00:00:52,489 --> 00:00:53,931
stock market?

22
00:00:56,717 --> 00:00:59,983
Wirecard was founded In 1999,

23
00:00:59,983 --> 00:01:00,874
in Aschheim,

24
00:01:00,874 --> 00:01:02,450
a suburb of Munich,

25
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:04,877
during the first internet boom.

26
00:01:05,047 --> 00:01:06,816
Its job sounded simple:

27
00:01:06,816 --> 00:01:09,627
process card payments for websites.

28
00:01:09,877 --> 00:01:12,242
Its early customers were businesses many

29
00:01:12,242 --> 00:01:13,316
banks saw as high risk,

30
00:01:13,316 --> 00:01:15,607
such as online gambling and adult sites.

31
00:01:15,607 --> 00:01:17,474
In 2005,

32
00:01:17,474 --> 00:01:19,723
it got onto the stock market without

33
00:01:19,723 --> 00:01:21,049
a classic listing,

34
00:01:21,049 --> 00:01:23,523
by merging into a company that was

35
00:01:23,523 --> 00:01:25,922
already listed in Frankfurt.

36
00:01:26,183 --> 00:01:28,874
Let me draw what a payment processor

37
00:01:28,874 --> 00:01:29,683
does.

38
00:01:29,683 --> 00:01:32,001
You buy something online and tap your

39
00:01:32,001 --> 00:01:32,683
card.

40
00:01:32,683 --> 00:01:34,428
The shop sends the payment to a

41
00:01:34,428 --> 00:01:35,683
processor.

42
00:01:35,713 --> 00:01:37,207
The processor checks it,

43
00:01:37,207 --> 00:01:38,939
talks to the card network and the

44
00:01:38,939 --> 00:01:39,351
banks,

45
00:01:39,351 --> 00:01:41,683
and makes sure the shop gets paid.

46
00:01:41,863 --> 00:01:42,897
For each payment,

47
00:01:42,897 --> 00:01:44,763
it keeps a tiny fee.

48
00:01:44,763 --> 00:01:47,158
Millions of tiny fees can become a

49
00:01:47,158 --> 00:01:49,195
very big business.

50
00:01:49,450 --> 00:01:51,242
And Wirecard grew fast,

51
00:01:51,242 --> 00:01:53,660
especially far from home.

52
00:01:53,910 --> 00:01:55,477
By early twenty sixteen,

53
00:01:55,477 --> 00:01:57,897
about 40 percent of its revenue came

54
00:01:57,897 --> 00:02:00,480
from Southeast Asia and the Middle East.

55
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:03,216
In twenty seventeen it reported revenue of

56
00:02:03,216 --> 00:02:05,820
more than 1.1 billion dollars for the

57
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:06,830
first time.

58
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:07,802
By then,

59
00:02:07,802 --> 00:02:10,465
its shares had risen roughly eight hundred

60
00:02:10,465 --> 00:02:12,885
percent since its stock market debut in

61
00:02:12,885 --> 00:02:14,453
twenty oh five.

62
00:02:14,717 --> 00:02:17,197
Then came the big moment.

63
00:02:17,197 --> 00:02:19,856
In September 2018,

64
00:02:19,856 --> 00:02:21,950
Wirecard entered the DAX,

65
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:24,750
Germany's index of its top companies,

66
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:26,845
and it pushed out Commerzbank,

67
00:02:26,845 --> 00:02:29,167
one of the country's biggest banks.

68
00:02:29,167 --> 00:02:32,988
Wirecard had fewer than 5,000 employees,

69
00:02:32,988 --> 00:02:34,371
a tenth of the bank's,

70
00:02:34,371 --> 00:02:36,357
but was worth twice as much.

71
00:02:36,357 --> 00:02:37,221
At its peak,

72
00:02:37,221 --> 00:02:39,925
it was valued at about 28 billion

73
00:02:39,925 --> 00:02:40,968
dollars.

74
00:02:41,217 --> 00:02:43,643
A German fintech champion,

75
00:02:43,643 --> 00:02:44,857
at last.

76
00:02:44,887 --> 00:02:47,578
But some people were already asking a

77
00:02:47,578 --> 00:02:48,851
simple question:

78
00:02:48,851 --> 00:02:51,297
where does all this money actually come

79
00:02:51,297 --> 00:02:52,142
from?

80
00:02:57,783 --> 00:03:00,253
Who saw the problems first?

81
00:03:00,253 --> 00:03:03,036
And why did almost nobody listen?

82
00:03:06,783 --> 00:03:08,520
In 2015,

83
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:11,010
a journalist at the Financial Times,

84
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:11,617
Dan McCrum,

85
00:03:11,617 --> 00:03:13,919
began asking questions about Wirecard's

86
00:03:13,919 --> 00:03:16,193
acquisitions and its accounts.

87
00:03:16,493 --> 00:03:18,448
In January twenty nineteen,

88
00:03:18,448 --> 00:03:20,233
the paper went further.

89
00:03:20,353 --> 00:03:22,102
Based on a whistleblower,

90
00:03:22,102 --> 00:03:24,264
a former head of legal for Wirecard

91
00:03:24,264 --> 00:03:25,031
in Asia,

92
00:03:25,031 --> 00:03:27,586
it reported suspicions about the company's

93
00:03:27,586 --> 00:03:29,562
business in Singapore.

94
00:03:29,817 --> 00:03:32,777
What happened next was extraordinary.

95
00:03:32,777 --> 00:03:34,942
Germany's financial regulator,

96
00:03:34,942 --> 00:03:35,518
BaFin,

97
00:03:35,518 --> 00:03:37,637
did not turn on the company.

98
00:03:37,637 --> 00:03:40,288
It banned investors from betting against

99
00:03:40,288 --> 00:03:42,433
Wirecard's shares for two months,

100
00:03:42,433 --> 00:03:44,361
the first time it had ever done

101
00:03:44,361 --> 00:03:46,407
that for a single stock.

102
00:03:46,707 --> 00:03:48,935
And prosecutors in Munich opened an

103
00:03:48,935 --> 00:03:51,348
investigation into a Financial Times

104
00:03:51,348 --> 00:03:52,072
journalist,

105
00:03:52,072 --> 00:03:54,157
after a criminal complaint.

106
00:03:54,177 --> 00:03:57,299
The newspaper rejected the allegations as

107
00:03:57,299 --> 00:03:59,100
baseless and false.

108
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:02,580
Let me draw how strange that was.

109
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:03,428
On one side,

110
00:04:03,428 --> 00:04:05,276
the journalists and the short sellers,

111
00:04:05,276 --> 00:04:05,687
saying:

112
00:04:05,687 --> 00:04:06,970
look at these numbers.

113
00:04:06,970 --> 00:04:08,484
On the other,

114
00:04:08,484 --> 00:04:09,997
the company,

115
00:04:09,997 --> 00:04:10,677
saying:

116
00:04:10,677 --> 00:04:13,130
we are being attacked.

117
00:04:13,170 --> 00:04:14,335
And in the middle,

118
00:04:14,335 --> 00:04:16,508
the watchdog that is supposed to protect

119
00:04:16,508 --> 00:04:17,700
investors.

120
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:20,997
It pointed its finger at the critics.

121
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:23,560
So the critics were wrong?

122
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:24,495
To find out,

123
00:04:24,495 --> 00:04:26,414
someone had to go and find the

124
00:04:26,414 --> 00:04:27,493
money.

125
00:04:33,150 --> 00:04:36,910
Where was Wirecard's money supposed to be?

126
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,806
And why couldn't anyone see it?

127
00:04:42,150 --> 00:04:44,901
An important part of Wirecard's business

128
00:04:44,901 --> 00:04:46,735
ran through so-called third-party

129
00:04:46,735 --> 00:04:47,469
partners:

130
00:04:47,469 --> 00:04:50,220
other companies in Asia and the Middle

131
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:52,054
East that processed payments for

132
00:04:52,054 --> 00:04:54,030
Wirecard's customers.

133
00:04:54,190 --> 00:04:56,440
Wirecard booked the revenue.

134
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,513
And the cash from that business,

135
00:04:58,513 --> 00:04:59,162
it said,

136
00:04:59,162 --> 00:05:00,931
sat in special trust accounts,

137
00:05:00,931 --> 00:05:03,099
held for it by a trustee.

138
00:05:03,350 --> 00:05:05,185
In April twenty twenty,

139
00:05:05,185 --> 00:05:08,205
a special investigation by the auditor

140
00:05:08,205 --> 00:05:11,360
KPMG was supposed to settle the question.

141
00:05:11,590 --> 00:05:12,940
It did not.

142
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:15,594
According to Wirecard's own statement,

143
00:05:15,594 --> 00:05:17,481
not all the data needed to prove

144
00:05:17,481 --> 00:05:19,787
the revenues of that business could be

145
00:05:19,787 --> 00:05:20,604
obtained,

146
00:05:20,604 --> 00:05:22,281
because it was in the hands of

147
00:05:22,281 --> 00:05:23,510
the partners.

148
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:24,499
Wirecard,

149
00:05:24,499 --> 00:05:25,358
for its part,

150
00:05:25,358 --> 00:05:27,560
said the investigation had found no

151
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:30,202
incriminating evidence of balance sheet

152
00:05:30,202 --> 00:05:31,463
forgery.

153
00:05:31,717 --> 00:05:32,207
Then,

154
00:05:32,207 --> 00:05:33,470
on June eighteenth,

155
00:05:33,470 --> 00:05:34,733
twenty twenty,

156
00:05:34,733 --> 00:05:37,285
Wirecard's regular auditor,

157
00:05:37,285 --> 00:05:37,775
EY,

158
00:05:37,775 --> 00:05:40,437
refused to sign the accounts.

159
00:05:40,507 --> 00:05:43,840
Cash worth about 2.2 billion dollars could

160
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:45,657
not be confirmed.

161
00:05:45,737 --> 00:05:47,061
There were indications,

162
00:05:47,061 --> 00:05:48,193
the company said,

163
00:05:48,193 --> 00:05:49,920
that a trustee had tried to deceive

164
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:51,287
the auditor.

165
00:05:51,487 --> 00:05:52,227
That day,

166
00:05:52,227 --> 00:05:54,268
the shares fell by as much as

167
00:05:54,268 --> 00:05:56,195
71 percent.

168
00:05:56,450 --> 00:05:58,700
The search ended in the Philippines,

169
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:00,764
where the money was supposed to be

170
00:06:00,764 --> 00:06:02,150
held in two banks.

171
00:06:02,150 --> 00:06:04,350
Both said the documents were false,

172
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:06,823
and that Wirecard was not even a

173
00:06:06,823 --> 00:06:07,670
client.

174
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,843
The Philippine central bank said the money

175
00:06:09,843 --> 00:06:11,350
did not seem to have entered the

176
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:12,620
country at all.

177
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:14,209
Four days later,

178
00:06:14,209 --> 00:06:16,200
Wirecard admitted it:

179
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:19,470
the money most likely did not exist.

180
00:06:19,610 --> 00:06:21,715
That was about a quarter of everything

181
00:06:21,715 --> 00:06:23,718
the company said it owned.

182
00:06:23,983 --> 00:06:27,071
A company worth billions had built its

183
00:06:27,071 --> 00:06:27,966
success,

184
00:06:27,966 --> 00:06:29,479
at least in part,

185
00:06:29,479 --> 00:06:31,623
on cash that wasn't there.

186
00:06:31,853 --> 00:06:34,794
What happened next took one week.

187
00:06:40,450 --> 00:06:44,363
How fast can a star company fall?

188
00:06:49,450 --> 00:06:50,850
Very fast.

189
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:52,087
On June nineteenth,

190
00:06:52,087 --> 00:06:53,533
the chief executive,

191
00:06:53,533 --> 00:06:54,351
Markus Braun,

192
00:06:54,351 --> 00:06:55,580
resigned.

193
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:56,849
On June twenty-fifth,

194
00:06:56,849 --> 00:06:59,600
Wirecard filed for insolvency.

195
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:01,615
It became the first company in the

196
00:07:01,615 --> 00:07:03,767
DAX ever to go out of business,

197
00:07:03,767 --> 00:07:06,255
leaving creditors owed nearly 4 billion

198
00:07:06,255 --> 00:07:07,250
dollars.

199
00:07:07,270 --> 00:07:10,133
A company once valued at 28 billion

200
00:07:10,133 --> 00:07:11,600
dollars was gone.

201
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:12,884
Two years earlier,

202
00:07:12,884 --> 00:07:15,205
it had pushed one of Germany's biggest

203
00:07:15,205 --> 00:07:16,780
banks out of the DAX.

204
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:19,729
Now it had fallen out of existence.

205
00:07:19,983 --> 00:07:22,523
And one man vanished.

206
00:07:22,623 --> 00:07:23,687
Jan Marsalek,

207
00:07:23,687 --> 00:07:26,271
Wirecard's chief operating officer,

208
00:07:26,271 --> 00:07:29,043
is wanted by German authorities.

209
00:07:29,233 --> 00:07:30,763
His whereabouts are unknown,

210
00:07:30,763 --> 00:07:32,506
but he is believed to be in

211
00:07:32,506 --> 00:07:33,493
Russia.

212
00:07:33,703 --> 00:07:35,251
In twenty twenty-five,

213
00:07:35,251 --> 00:07:37,718
a court in London convicted members of

214
00:07:37,718 --> 00:07:40,113
a spy ring working for Russia.

215
00:07:40,253 --> 00:07:42,297
According to British prosecutors,

216
00:07:42,297 --> 00:07:44,773
its leader reported to Marsalek.

217
00:07:44,953 --> 00:07:46,841
Between twenty twenty and twenty

218
00:07:46,841 --> 00:07:47,659
twenty-three,

219
00:07:47,659 --> 00:07:48,896
prosecutors said,

220
00:07:48,896 --> 00:07:51,203
the group gathered information about

221
00:07:51,203 --> 00:07:53,510
people and places of interest to the

222
00:07:53,510 --> 00:07:54,739
Russian state.

223
00:07:54,983 --> 00:07:57,450
Markus Braun went on trial in Munich

224
00:07:57,450 --> 00:08:00,547
in December 2022,

225
00:08:00,547 --> 00:08:03,260
accused of commercial gang fraud,

226
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:06,053
false accounting and breach of trust.

227
00:08:06,253 --> 00:08:07,224
If convicted,

228
00:08:07,224 --> 00:08:09,247
he faces up to 15 years in

229
00:08:09,247 --> 00:08:10,243
prison.

230
00:08:10,453 --> 00:08:12,505
He rejects all the charges,

231
00:08:12,505 --> 00:08:14,923
and has blamed Marsalek.

232
00:08:15,213 --> 00:08:17,250
The trial has gone on for years:

233
00:08:17,250 --> 00:08:19,735
the court set new hearing dates until

234
00:08:19,735 --> 00:08:22,323
the end of twenty twenty-six.

235
00:08:22,623 --> 00:08:24,184
Until there is a verdict,

236
00:08:24,184 --> 00:08:26,365
he is presumed innocent.

237
00:08:26,617 --> 00:08:28,427
But a fraud of this size does

238
00:08:28,427 --> 00:08:31,531
not happen without many people looking the

239
00:08:31,531 --> 00:08:32,817
other way.

240
00:08:32,817 --> 00:08:34,603
So who failed?

241
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:41,118
Auditors,

242
00:08:41,118 --> 00:08:42,208
regulators,

243
00:08:42,208 --> 00:08:43,143
investors:

244
00:08:43,143 --> 00:08:45,523
who was supposed to catch it?

245
00:08:49,250 --> 00:08:49,810
First,

246
00:08:49,810 --> 00:08:51,590
the auditor.

247
00:08:51,780 --> 00:08:54,123
EY had signed off Wirecard's accounts

248
00:08:54,123 --> 00:08:57,300
without objections for more than a decade.

249
00:08:57,500 --> 00:08:59,036
In twenty twenty-three,

250
00:08:59,036 --> 00:09:01,485
the German audit watchdog banned it from

251
00:09:01,485 --> 00:09:03,711
taking on new listed audit clients in

252
00:09:03,711 --> 00:09:05,247
Germany for two years,

253
00:09:05,247 --> 00:09:07,696
and fined the firm and several current

254
00:09:07,696 --> 00:09:09,409
and former employees.

255
00:09:09,650 --> 00:09:10,184
Then,

256
00:09:10,184 --> 00:09:12,150
the regulator.

257
00:09:12,150 --> 00:09:14,766
In January 2021,

258
00:09:14,766 --> 00:09:16,131
the head of BaFin,

259
00:09:16,131 --> 00:09:17,266
Felix Hufeld,

260
00:09:17,266 --> 00:09:18,560
left his post.

261
00:09:18,590 --> 00:09:20,795
The finance minister at the time,

262
00:09:20,795 --> 00:09:21,663
Olaf Scholz,

263
00:09:21,663 --> 00:09:23,445
said it would allow a fresh start

264
00:09:23,445 --> 00:09:26,340
at an organisation that had overlooked

265
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:28,567
Germany's greatest postwar accounting

266
00:09:28,567 --> 00:09:29,390
fraud.

267
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:31,949
His deputy in charge of securities

268
00:09:31,949 --> 00:09:33,873
supervision left too.

269
00:09:34,117 --> 00:09:36,157
Let me draw the safety net that

270
00:09:36,157 --> 00:09:38,117
was supposed to work.

271
00:09:38,147 --> 00:09:40,247
The company reports its numbers.

272
00:09:40,247 --> 00:09:41,777
The auditor checks them.

273
00:09:41,777 --> 00:09:44,027
The regulator watches the market.

274
00:09:44,107 --> 00:09:46,602
Journalists and investors ask hard

275
00:09:46,602 --> 00:09:47,757
questions.

276
00:09:47,767 --> 00:09:48,983
At Wirecard,

277
00:09:48,983 --> 00:09:49,951
the last line,

278
00:09:49,951 --> 00:09:50,918
the critics,

279
00:09:50,918 --> 00:09:53,007
was the one that worked.

280
00:09:53,027 --> 00:09:54,367
The official checks,

281
00:09:54,367 --> 00:09:56,388
the ones everyone trusted,

282
00:09:56,388 --> 00:09:57,669
came too late.

283
00:09:57,917 --> 00:09:59,686
The lesson is not only about one

284
00:09:59,686 --> 00:10:00,817
company.

285
00:10:00,927 --> 00:10:03,059
When a story is exciting,

286
00:10:03,059 --> 00:10:04,712
a national champion,

287
00:10:04,712 --> 00:10:05,886
a tech success,

288
00:10:05,886 --> 00:10:07,300
a rising share price,

289
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:09,577
people want to believe it.

290
00:10:09,797 --> 00:10:11,633
And the questions that could ruin the

291
00:10:11,633 --> 00:10:14,158
story are the ones nobody wants to

292
00:10:14,158 --> 00:10:14,987
ask.

293
00:10:15,267 --> 00:10:17,730
Every check in the system assumed that

294
00:10:17,730 --> 00:10:20,117
someone else had already checked.

295
00:10:20,117 --> 00:10:21,105
And for years,

296
00:10:21,105 --> 00:10:23,384
the people who did ask were treated

297
00:10:23,384 --> 00:10:24,997
as the problem.

298
00:10:25,250 --> 00:10:25,746
So,

299
00:10:25,746 --> 00:10:28,881
the two billion dollars that never existed

300
00:10:28,881 --> 00:10:31,280
were not stolen from a vault.

301
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:32,930
They were never in one.

302
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:34,796
They lived in documents,

303
00:10:34,796 --> 00:10:36,592
in trust and in confidence,

304
00:10:36,592 --> 00:10:39,920
until someone finally asked to see them.

305
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:41,304
And when they looked,

306
00:10:41,304 --> 00:10:43,010
there was nothing there.

307
00:10:43,010 --> 00:10:45,576
The most expensive question in this story

308
00:10:45,576 --> 00:10:47,489
was also the simplest one:

309
00:10:47,489 --> 00:10:49,488
can I see the money?

310
00:10:49,750 --> 00:10:52,053
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

311
00:10:52,053 --> 00:10:54,800
we follow the money behind another hidden

312
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:57,090
link of the world economy.

313
00:10:57,250 --> 00:10:59,618
This is The Hidden Economics.
